  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Advisory level heat continues today, with high \nhumidity lingering through the weekend.\n\nAs expected, temperatures tonight remain elevated.... right \naround 74 for a low. Dewpoints are also remaining elevated, \naround 72, and a layer of fog has developed along the South \nCoast, Cape/Islands, and in the CT River Valley. Similar to \nyesterday, it'll take a bit of sun power to burn off given how \ntropical an airmass we have sitting over us. Temperatures are \nexpected to surge again today, topping out in the upper 80s to \nlow 90s, with 925mb temps around +25C this afternoon. With the \nprolonged southerly flow, dewpoints will be on the rise again \ntoday as well... right around 73-74, leading to \"feels like\" \ntemperatures to soar into the upper 90s, near 100 at times. Also\nexpecting little to no relief tonight as temperatures will once\nagain remain in the low 70s with dewpoints not much lower.\n\nSaturday and Sunday look to be just slightly cooler, with \ntemperatures around 2-4 degrees less. Unfortunately, the \nhumidity will hang around through the weekend, leading to \"feels\nlike\" temperatures to remain in at least the low 90s.\n\nHeat Advisories remain in effect through at least this evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Diurnal shower and thunderstorm chances remain \na threat through Saturday, with dry weather returning Sunday. \nUnsettled weather may return early next week, as well as \ncontinuing high humidity.\n\nWhile a low-level warm layer will likely keep most storms from \nfiring during the day, CAM guidance is suggesting a possible \nline of thunderstorms moving through the western/interior \nportion of southern New England later this evening/tonight. \nNotably, the HRRR Neural Network's 03z run has significantly \nreduced chances for severe storms as far east as Hartford/CT \nRiver Valley compared to the 00z run. Highest chances of severe \nthunderstorms remain in northern NJ/NYC metro, but cannot rule \nout 1 or 2 possibly severe storms in western CT/MA tonight.\n\nSaturday still looks to have the best chances for scattered \nthunderstorms across southern New England, with roughly the same\nset- up as today. Highest chances will once again be in the \nlate evening/post-sunset hours, with model guidance honing in on\na more robust line pushing through the NYC metro and \nsouthern/western portion of CT.\n\nAny thunderstorms that do pop both today and Saturday have the \npotential to produce frequent lightning, gusty winds, and \ntorrential downpours. PWATs will likely be near 2.0\" off and on \nthrough the weekend. Model soundings also indicate the mid-level\nsteering flow could be rather week, leading to slow moving \nstorms or even training. Given the amount of rain southern New \nEngland has seen over the last week or two, any heavy downpours \ncould lead to localized flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather may return early next week, \nas well as continuing high humidity.\n\nExpecting a few more shortwaves to move through the upper-level\nflow as well as a trailing cold front early next week, bringing\nadditional chances for showers to southern New England Monday \nnight into Tuesday. The FROPA could bring a small amount of \nrelief from the heat for the start of the next work week, \nknocking dewpoints down to the high 60s instead of mid 70s, but \nhumidity levels likely will remain elevated through mid-week.",
